#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151983 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152286 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ebb2332-3e40-5627-bc43-258ca4a80424" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Imaginary Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "88fe37cf-5df2-5988-90b8-082f7aaca609" :question/math-academy-id "e-6791" :question/problem "Solve the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny'' + y = 0, \\quad y(\\pi) = 2, \\quad y'(\\pi) = -1\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\ny = A \\cos x + B \\sin x\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find constants $A$ and $B.$ To do this, we apply the initial conditions $y(\\pi)=2,$ $y'(\\pi)=-1.$\n\n- Substituting $y(\\pi) = 2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & =2 \\\\ A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi  & =2 \\\\ -A & =2 \\\\ A & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Computing $y'(x),$ we get\n\n$$\ny' =  - A \\sin{x} + B\\cos{x},\n$$\n\nand substituting $y'(\\pi)=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\pi ) & =-1 \\\\ -A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  & =-1 \\\\ -B & =-1 \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = -2\\cos{x} + \\sin{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151994 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b627c23d-f87b-5fd9-8e53-f2464eecff4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Distinct Real Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "4739ced1-bbcc-516b-971f-a580ec97961a" :question/math-academy-id "e-6861" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + y - 2y =0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' + y - 2y =0,\n\\ \\ y(1) = e, \\ \\ y'(1) = e ^{-2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1)=e$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e & =A{e}^{1}+B{e}^{-2(1)} \\\\ e & =Ae+B{e}^{-2} \\\\ A & =1-B{e}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(1) = e^{-2},$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\ny' = Ae^x - 2Be^{-x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(1)=e^{-2}$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2} & =A{e}^{1}-2B{e}^{-2(1)} \\\\ {e}^{-2} & =Ae-2B{e}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A=1-B{e}^{-3} \\\\ {e}^{-2}=Ae-2B{e}^{-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A = 1 - B e ^{-3}$ in the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2} & =(1-B{e}^{-3})e-2B{e}^{-2} \\\\ 1 & ={e}^{3}-B-2B \\\\ B & =\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting $B= \\dfrac {e ^ 3 - 1} 3$ back into the equation $A = 1-Be^{-3}$ gives\n\n$$\nA = 1 -  \\dfrac {(e ^ 3 - 1) e ^ {-3}} 3 =   \\dfrac {2 +  e ^ {-3}} 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution of the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny =  \\dfrac {  e ^ {-3} + 2} 3  e^{x} + \\dfrac {e ^ 3 - 1} 3 e^{-2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151995 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84e87d0d-806d-5587-849e-17d6999dbbd1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Repeated Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "95cdd4cc-df70-5dac-8bd3-66edb012cdcb" :question/math-academy-id "e-6862" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' - 4 y' + 4y =0,\n\\qquad y(0) =  3, \\qquad y'(0) = 2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have a repeated root $\\lambda = 2,$ so we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\ny = A e ^ { 2 x} + B x e ^ {2 x}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=3$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =A{e}^{2(0)}+B(0){e}^{2(0)} \\\\ 3 & =A{e}^{0}+0 \\\\ 3 & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y' (0) = 2,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\ny'  = 2 A e ^ {2 x} +  B e ^ { 2 x} + 2 B x e ^{ 2 x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(0)=2$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2A{e}^{2(0)}+B{e}^{2(0)}+2B(0){e}^{2(0)} \\\\ 2 & =2A{e}^{0}+B{e}^{0}+0 \\\\ 2 & =2A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3=A \\\\ 2=2A+B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=3$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =6+B \\\\ B & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=3$ and $B=-4,$ and therefore the solution is \n\n$$\ny = 3 e ^ { 2 x } - 4 x e ^ { 2 x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152008 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152287 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20e5f532-d99f-5406-b006-f34aa9074819" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "80968044-25e2-5906-929c-b944f84ca27d" :question/math-academy-id "e-7051" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' - 6y' + 25y=0, \\quad y(0)= 2,  \\quad y'(0) = 5, \\quad t\\gt 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9)-9+25 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-3)^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda -3 & =4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =3\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\ny = e^{3x} \\left( P \\cos 4x + Q \\sin 4x \\right)\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0) = 2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n2 = P.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(0) = 5,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3{e}^{3x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)+{e}^{3x}(-4P\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+4Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(0) = 5$ and $P=2$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =3{e}^{0}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)+{e}^{0}(-4(2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+4Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\ 5 & =6+4Q \\\\ -1 & =4Q \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = e^{3x} \\left( 2 \\cos 4x - \\dfrac{1}{4} \\sin 4x \\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152401 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b627c23d-f87b-5fd9-8e53-f2464eecff4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Distinct Real Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93ff3f4b-4122-5834-9abc-6dc6cbcdbfb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = e^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148938} {:answer/id #uuid "ff989b86-9f5f-50dc-ac73-7bdcdebc2ca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2e^{-x} - e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148939} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd5d053-4ee7-5914-a6b3-2c9a24d5285e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148940} {:answer/id #uuid "2d57ef09-f4d2-59c9-8314-850f109cf906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 4e^{-x} - 3e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148941} {:answer/id #uuid "51b7252f-f90c-5e18-bc65-c27f9d042d3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-e^{-x} + 2e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148942}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3cd7eb07-6ad6-5159-a544-24234ed2ccad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148937}] :question/id #uuid "93cffb5e-8615-53c1-a574-533e068512ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-130343" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n$y^{″} - y^{′} - 2y = 0, y(0) = 1, y^{′}(0) =-4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152402 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84e87d0d-806d-5587-849e-17d6999dbbd1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9de4a8b-74dd-55f9-81a0-00efc0c803cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-2e^{-3x} - xe^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148944} {:answer/id #uuid "bd9c5edf-9e7d-559a-bd7f-51330e7f2038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-2e^{3x} - xe^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148945} {:answer/id #uuid "335565f2-f073-5a7f-8e9c-432011952dfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-2e^{3x} + 2xe^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148946} {:answer/id #uuid "62dda67d-7cf3-5e5c-900a-bfd4c930e509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-2e^{-3x} + 8xe^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148947} {:answer/id #uuid "7237a62f-f860-55ce-a521-1bf5014f4383" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-2e^{-3x} - 4xe^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148948}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e82527bc-a697-52b6-866e-a3ae6fadb46e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148943}] :question/id #uuid "da0e1bd5-5ab3-52c5-b411-da88433bbf6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-130346" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n$y^{″} + 6y^{′} + 9y = 0, y(0) =-2, y^{′}(0) = 2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265892 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152286 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ebb2332-3e40-5627-bc43-258ca4a80424" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Imaginary Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bfe40da-51cf-5cfc-8bf3-d8d61eb492c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡12x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡12x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263115} {:answer/id #uuid "41137a18-3b7b-5a66-99b6-7227a71e6489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡12x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263578} {:answer/id #uuid "45cd59f9-c985-59bb-b396-6d5cf9a8a8eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡12x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡12x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263613} {:answer/id #uuid "a7a7c280-30ea-5792-a4e6-5f3be8233ffd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡12x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡12x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264434} {:answer/id #uuid "e54d24eb-37ad-5a49-959c-d7dc616c5073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡12x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bfe40da-51cf-5cfc-8bf3-d8d61eb492c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡12x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡12x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263115} :answer-field/id #uuid "5179e9c4-31c5-5342-81c7-5cb9365f2374" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c1d3d599-7dfd-5eae-928a-1ef2cf2f900f" :question/math-academy-id "q-130356" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+144y=0,\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=1,\\,{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=24.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+144 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-144 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 12\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =P\\operatorname{cos}⁡12x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡12x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-Q=1\\,⟹\\,Q=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=24,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-12P\\operatorname{sin}⁡12x+12Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡12x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=24$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12P=24\\,⟹\\,P=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡12x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡12x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152777 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b627c23d-f87b-5fd9-8e53-f2464eecff4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Distinct Real Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dabdd0c-2588-5f7d-8ccd-b68af2421ea7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=7{e}^{-3x}-10{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342485} {:answer/id #uuid "49169b95-990f-59eb-8bb6-9cf98419ab8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{e}^{-3x}-6{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342839} {:answer/id #uuid "a4feee5b-c504-529a-b361-ad69aa51b813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-12{e}^{-3x}+9{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343692} {:answer/id #uuid "c6148395-0be0-5c18-b79a-ca9ffdf1bf78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-10{e}^{-3x}+7{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343975} {:answer/id #uuid "eb97d4ca-37d6-530d-8bb6-f0f612d82f07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-9{e}^{-3x}+6{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6148395-0be0-5c18-b79a-ca9ffdf1bf78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-10{e}^{-3x}+7{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343975} :answer-field/id #uuid "18454ee3-e909-5372-948f-8f633a9d0c9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344531}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "de2c9bcd-a587-5660-97a8-aa4955c427cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-7788" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+12y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+12y=0,\\,y(0)=-3,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=-3$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B=-3.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=2,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3A{e}^{-3x}-4B{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=2$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n-3A-4B=2.\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=-3 \\\\ -3A-4B=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-10,B=7.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-10{e}^{-3x}+7{e}^{-4x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393765 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84e87d0d-806d-5587-849e-17d6999dbbd1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eeaa8919-6cd1-523d-89da-7ef2030fd077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=11{e}^{4x-4}-9x{e}^{4x-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393767} {:answer/id #uuid "31ad293a-a2de-5b04-a010-75b1e574916b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-7{e}^{4x-4}+9x{e}^{4x-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393768} {:answer/id #uuid "0a7466a7-5e6b-57ce-b40f-1d86f392fa6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5{e}^{4x-4}-3x{e}^{4x-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393769} {:answer/id #uuid "2731b0a9-3ca5-56a5-b3d2-8efcf3d3f0af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=8{e}^{2x-2}-6x{e}^{2x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393770} {:answer/id #uuid "738f5cba-3a2c-5ec8-92f7-05586e62d819" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{e}^{2x-2}-x{e}^{2x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393771}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eeaa8919-6cd1-523d-89da-7ef2030fd077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=11{e}^{4x-4}-9x{e}^{4x-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393767} :answer-field/id #uuid "656ffe0f-9fb3-55a1-98b5-b13db8a4f14b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0ccd8f3-5c55-5b1e-8569-1f7045c0f609" :question/math-academy-id "q-7798" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-8{y}^{′}+16y=0,\\,y(1)=2,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(1)=-1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-8\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+16{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +16) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -4) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -4)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =4$ is a repeated root.\n\nSo the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}+Bx{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nLet us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(1)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA{e}^{4}+B{e}^{4}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=-1.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{4x}+4Bx{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n4A{e}^{4}+5B{e}^{4}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B$:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{4}+B{e}^{4}=2 \\\\ 4A{e}^{4}+5B{e}^{4}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=11{e}^{-4},B=-9{e}^{-4}.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =11{e}^{-4}\\cdot {e}^{4x}-9{e}^{-4}\\cdot x{e}^{4x} \\\\  & =11{e}^{4x-4}-9x{e}^{4x-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84e87d0d-806d-5587-849e-17d6999dbbd1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29e8157a-552f-5c3e-9922-5f98c42236bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{x/2-1}+3x{e}^{x/2-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345723} {:answer/id #uuid "2aa6a344-1c11-5d1d-91e2-7b17d052e60c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x{e}^{-3x/2+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345730} {:answer/id #uuid "3fb0744b-1328-5467-aff8-5eda9102546f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-3x/2+1}+2x{e}^{-3x/2+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345893} {:answer/id #uuid "5e98c6cd-0b58-542c-859e-0b8cec34dba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{x/2-1}+2x{e}^{x/2-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346167} {:answer/id #uuid "8abde012-2b17-5b1a-afc7-33d35b8535b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{e}^{-3x/2+1}-2x{e}^{-3x/2+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346552}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fb0744b-1328-5467-aff8-5eda9102546f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-3x/2+1}+2x{e}^{-3x/2+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345893} :answer-field/id #uuid "db589b01-39df-5ab0-9143-384f9d7d07fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ece2165a-867c-5bfa-9095-1c580456d3cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-7815" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+\\frac{9y}{4}=0,\\,y(1)={e}^{-1/2},\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-1/2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+3\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+\\frac{9}{4}{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +\\frac{9}{4}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +\\frac{9}{4} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda +\\frac{3}{2})(\\lambda +\\frac{3}{2}) & =0 \\\\ {(\\lambda +\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =\\frac{3}{2}$ is a repeated root.\n\nSo the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x/2}+Bx{e}^{-3x/2}.\n$$\n\nLet us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \nSubstituting $y(1)={e}^{-1/2}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-3/2}+B{e}^{-3/2} & ={e}^{-1/2} \\\\ A+B & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-1/2}.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{3}{2}A{e}^{-3/2x}+B{e}^{-3/2x}-\\frac{3}{2}Bx{e}^{-3/2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-1/2}$ into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}A{e}^{-3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}B{e}^{-3/2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-1/2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}A-\\frac{1}{2}B & =\\frac{1}{2}e \\\\ -3A-B & =e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B$:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=e \\\\ -3A-B=e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-e,B=2e.$\n\nNow we get the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-e\\cdot {e}^{-3x/2}+2e\\cdot x{e}^{-3x/2} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x/2+1}+2x{e}^{-3x/2+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348131 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152287 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20e5f532-d99f-5406-b006-f34aa9074819" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f9690a7-a7e2-5397-9fab-5932d5f57697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{2x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345480} {:answer/id #uuid "259386fa-930c-5ed2-8146-0410d65c7c78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345690} {:answer/id #uuid "3e1db9ec-13d0-523f-beab-84f5738731bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345876} {:answer/id #uuid "d42af497-46f2-5618-b60b-d642f0f7e4c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347206} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d750ad-e2fd-5a5b-a423-66b337485f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6d750ad-e2fd-5a5b-a423-66b337485f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347247} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6288fe0-74a5-5333-b0d5-8d242026b44c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9131a7ff-7e61-5a90-96bc-6b1cab75b7bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-7892" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+8y=0,\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={e}^{\\pi },\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=4{e}^{\\pi }.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4)-4+8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda -2 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =2\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={e}^{\\pi }$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-P{e}^{\\pi } & ={e}^{\\pi } \\\\ P & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=4{e}^{\\pi },$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+{e}^{2x}(-2P\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=4{e}^{\\pi }$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2P{e}^{\\pi }-2Q{e}^{\\pi } & =4{e}^{\\pi } \\\\ -2P-2Q & =4 \\\\ 2-2Q & =4 \\\\ Q & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=-1$ and $Q=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{2x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152286 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ebb2332-3e40-5627-bc43-258ca4a80424" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Imaginary Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30e2251b-7d16-5372-af31-a605bcd8d61d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355091} {:answer/id #uuid "35368f75-5659-5ae7-a5af-a8c1c96b7648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355141} {:answer/id #uuid "5b39f1ae-f133-5b7b-a1db-74e10797637e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)-4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355470} {:answer/id #uuid "8ec40822-5e09-542f-9d33-f9368a95d302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355923} {:answer/id #uuid "a525c814-3a04-5b28-90af-15cdaca6df19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a525c814-3a04-5b28-90af-15cdaca6df19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356129} :answer-field/id #uuid "c79cbe3f-3a7d-5248-b7e4-045c51d53800" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "169defb3-7918-5f63-aa50-008ef745a1e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-94020" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=0,\\,y(0)=-4,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=4\\sqrt{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-2 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =P\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=-4$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=4\\sqrt{2},$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\sqrt{2}P\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+\\sqrt{2}Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=4\\sqrt{2}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}Q=4\\sqrt{2}\\,⟹\\,Q=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357627 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152287 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20e5f532-d99f-5406-b006-f34aa9074819" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0038ad00-cbef-555c-b4ef-4c68141d3844" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}(-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354658} {:answer/id #uuid "09bfb13a-5032-50df-8a5f-8f4a6c0a4bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-2x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354755} {:answer/id #uuid "2f5dcb2c-cea8-56b7-9456-0766f72b8509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355075} {:answer/id #uuid "49966142-bc9a-56bd-a8a1-fa56c7efffc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355318} {:answer/id #uuid "c19a7e5b-8d42-5925-8bf0-cfa378fb7bd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{-2x}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0038ad00-cbef-555c-b4ef-4c68141d3844" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}(-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354658} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc7a07ed-713b-5880-92ea-6cbd8e8d8cac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c3243450-c950-543f-968a-f0bf49df75ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-94025" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+5y=0,\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={e}^{-\\pi },\\,{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=6{e}^{-\\pi }.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4)-4+5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda +2 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{-2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={e}^{-\\pi }$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q{e}^{-\\pi } & ={e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ Q & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=6{e}^{-\\pi },$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2{e}^{-2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+{e}^{-2x}(-P\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=6{e}^{-\\pi }$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2Q{e}^{-\\pi }-P{e}^{-\\pi } & =6{e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ -2Q-P & =6 \\\\ -2-P & =6 \\\\ P & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=-8$ and $Q=1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{-2x}(-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
